\paragraph{Relaxation, Divergence, and SPON.}
At 50\%, Gumbel-Sigmoid outperforms STE~\cite{bengio2013ste} by 33.8\% (11.79 vs.\ 15.77); forward KL outperforms JSD (+6.4\%) and reverse KL (+8.6\%), consistent with mode-covering~\cite{gu2024minillm}. % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42, jsd_ablation_s42, reverse_kl_ablation_s42
At 30\%, forward KL (8.81) and JSD (8.90) are near-identical, both outperforming reverse KL (9.26) and dramatically better than MSE (18.52$\pm$1.68; $\sigma$=1.68 vs.\ KL's $\sigma$=0.02, reflecting per-layer signal instability). % exp_id: kl30_ablation_s42, jsd30_ablation_s42, rkl30_ablation_s42, mse30_uniform_s42, mse30_3seed
BCE without compensation (15.71) is consistent with decomposed losses underperforming at both regimes. % exp_id: bce30_uniform_s42
The near-equivalence of KL and JSD suggests the specific divergence measure matters less than whether the loss optimizes through the output distribution; within output-distribution losses, cross-layer scope provides downstream benefits not predicted by PPL alone (\S\ref{sec:perlayer}).
Reverse KL at 30\% (3-seed) shows the same asymmetry: commonsense exceeds TEAL (ARC-c 43.7\%, WG 69.0\%) while MMLU falls below (39.2\% vs.\ 40.7\% KL, 45.9\% TEAL). % exp_id: rkl30_downstream_3seed
\paragraph{Per-Layer KL: Decomposing Signal Type and Coupling Scope.}
\label{sec:perlayer}
To isolate cross-layer coupling, we train per-layer KL predictors that mask $K$ random layers per step and compute KL on the full model output, preserving the output-distribution signal without cross-layer gradients.
At $K{=}1$, per-layer KL achieves 9.05$\pm$0.02, within 2.7\% of end-to-end KL (8.81$\pm$0.02), while per-layer MSE degrades to 18.52$\pm$1.68 ($2.05\times$ worse). For PPL, signal type\footnote{We use ``signal type'' as shorthand for the training objective's optimization target (output distribution vs.\ intermediate activations); this dimension also entails differences in gradient flow topology and optimization landscape that are not independently controlled.} accounts for up to 97.5\% of the gap (upper bound; signal type and coupling scope are not fully orthogonal).
The $K{=}1$ downstream gap conflates true coupling with train--inference mismatch (training masks 1/32 layers; inference applies all 32). A $K{=}4$ pilot\footnote{Single seed (s42); actual sparsity 29.6\% vs.\ target 30\%.} suggests this confound is substantial and reveals task-dependent structure (Appendix~\ref{app:k4_downstream}): \emph{mismatch-dominated}---PPL (67\%) and BoolQ (73\%) gaps largely close with increased $K$; \emph{coupling-dominated}---ARC-c and WG retain substantial residual gap (60--73\% depending on $K{=}1$ baseline), indicating genuine cross-layer benefits; \emph{anomalous}---MMLU exceeds E2E KL (43.5 vs.\ 40.7$\pm$1.3, possibly training--eval alignment), while GSM8K and PIQA show no benefit. % exp_id: perlayer_kl_30_3seed, perlayer_kl_downstream, mse30_downstream_decomp, perlayer_kl_k4_30
At 50\% ($K{=}1$ only), the gap widens sharply: per-layer KL degrades to 34.85 vs.\ 11.92 for E2E KL (+192\%), with coupling's share rising from 2.5\% to 10.6\% ($4\times$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) and downstream falling to TEAL level. % exp_id: perlayer_kl_downstream, perlayer_kl_50_s42

\paragraph{STE Failure and Loss--Relaxation Interaction.}
\label{sec:ablation_ste}
At 30\%, STE yields only 20.8\% effective sparsity (vs.\ target 30\%), confirming Gumbel-Sigmoid is necessary. BCE+Gumbel (32.22) underperforms standard BCE (27.97); the loss effect ($\Delta$PPL=20.4) is $5.1\times$ the relaxation effect ($\Delta$PPL=3.98). % exp_id: ste_30pct_sparsity_failure, bce_gumbel_no_penalty_s42, bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation, eval_bce_v3_s42, bce_ste_s42

\paragraph{Compensation Redundancy.}
FastForward-style compensation~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} (+67.2M params) provides no benefit; robustness to $d_b$, temperature, and training steps confirmed in Appendices~\ref{app:comp_convergence}--\ref{app:training_convergence}.
For comparison, BCE with compensation (25.44 at 50\%) improves over BCE alone (27.96, +9.0\%) but remains $2.1\times$ worse than KL without compensation (11.92), indicating that compensation partially mitigates decomposed-loss deficits but cannot substitute for output-distribution training. % exp_id: kl_comp_30_s42, kl_comp_30_s456, kl_comp_50_s42, bce_comp_staged_s42, db_sensitivity_sweep, tau_schedule_sweep, training_steps_sweep


\subsection{Sparsity Scaling and Regime Analysis}
\label{sec:scaling}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_sparsity_sweep.pdf}
\vspace{-8pt}
\caption{PPL scaling with sparsity (global allocation shown; uniform recommended at $\leq$30\%; see \S\ref{sec:scaling}). KL's advantage grows: 14.5\% at 30\%, 50\% at 50\%, 82\% at 70\%.}
\label{fig:sparsity_sweep}
\vspace{-8pt}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Allocation Behavior.}
Table~\ref{tab:phase} (Appendix~\ref{app:alloc_full}) reveals non-monotonic allocation preference: uniform is best at $\leq$30\% ($7.3\times$ smaller MMLU $\sigma$), global at 35--40\%, both collapse at $\geq$45\%. % exp_id: kl30_3seed, regime_kl35_global, regime_kl35_uniform, multi_seed_kl50_global, multi_seed_kl50_uniform

\paragraph{Cross-Architecture and Scale.}
\label{sec:cross_arch}
On Mistral-7B-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral}, PPL generalizes ($-$17.3\%, 3-seed; Table~\ref{tab:mistral_downstream}); on Qwen-2.5-14B~\cite{qwen2024qwen25}, PPL generalizes but MMLU/GSM8K decline (Table~\ref{tab:14b}).
KL's mode-covering property may preserve distribution breadth at the expense of reasoning pathways; the consistent regression across scales (8B MMLU $-$5.2pp, 14B GSM8K $-$20pp) suggests a systematic property of distribution-matching objectives. % exp_id: mistral_kl30_s42, mistral_kl30_s123, mistral_kl30_s456, mistral_teal30_downstream, qwen14b_kl30_uniform_4task, qwen14b_teal30_uniform
The pipeline adds 6.4\% latency overhead (Appendix~\ref{app:latency}). % exp_id: latency_profiling

